-- Scene 1: the declaration is judged when it is DECLARED --
  [OK] an unknown TYPE raises at declaration time
  [OK] a typo'd KEY raises -- ':requird' cannot silently leave a field required
  [OK] a :oneof with no :choices raises (an empty enumeration is unsatisfiable)
  [OK] a :list that does not say :of what raises
  [OK] the same field declared twice raises (Ring folds keys -- they are ONE field)
  [OK] an unknown :must OPERATOR raises -- the graph-rule vocabulary is reused, so is its refusal
  [OK] an operator that cannot apply to the type raises ('>=' on a string)
  [OK] 'exists' in :must raises -- that is what :required already says
  [OK] a sound declaration compiles, and knows its own shape
  [OK] ...names its required fields
  [OK] ...and the compact form declares the same thing

-- Scene 2: two shapes are read, and nothing else is guessed at --
  [OK] JSON inside a fence inside prose is read
  [OK] the yaml-like MEMO shape is read too
  [OK] ...with its list intact
  [OK] prose that is not a structure is REFUSED, not scavenged
  [OK] ...and the refusal names the rule 'parse'

-- Scene 3: what comes back is the DECLARED shape --
  [OK] a representable scalar is COERCED: the string '36' arrives as the number 36
  [OK] ...and it really is a number, not a string that adds
  [OK] a closed enumeration folds case
  [OK] fields come back in DECLARED order, not the order the model wrote them
  [OK] a field nobody declared does NOT refuse the answer by default
  [OK] ...but it is reported
  [OK] ...as a WARNING, not an error
  [OK] ...and it is dropped from the whole
  [OK] RefuseUnknownFields() closes the language: the same extra field now refuses

-- Scene 4: partial credit is forbidden --
  [OK] one missing required field refuses the whole answer
  [OK] ...naming the rule
  [OK] ...and the field
  [OK] nothing partial escapes as a value
  [OK] a required field PRESENT BUT EMPTY is missing -- the model did not answer it
  [OK] a type mismatch refuses
  [OK] ...naming the rule 'type'
  [OK] a CLOSED enumeration is closed -- 'meh' is not near enough
  [OK] ...naming the rule 'oneof'
  [OK] a :must band refuses what falls outside it
  [OK] ...naming the OPERATOR as the rule
  [OK] ...and citing it in words a reader can act on

-- Scene 5: nesting -- structures inside structures, lists of them --
  [OK] a nested structure validates
  [OK] ...and reads back
  [OK] a list OF structures validates element by element
  [OK] ...each element coerced
  [OK] a field missing DEEP inside refuses the whole
  [OK] ...and the path says where: 'author.name'
  [OK] a comparison on a LIST speaks about its element count
  [OK] ...and says so in the message
  [OK] a defective element is located by index: 'steps[2].at'

-- Scene 6: the verdict joins the house CI gate --
  [OK] a finding carries :rule
  [OK] ...:subject
  [OK] ...:where
  [OK] ...:severity
  [OK] ...:message
  [OK] stzRuleReport ingests it without an adapter
  [OK] ...and the gate reads UNSOUND

-- Scene 7: retry inside the SAME budget, then refusal --
  [OK] the prompt CARRIES the shape -- the model is asked, not hoped at
  [OK] two malformed answers are retried and the third validates
  [OK] ...and the retries were paid for out of Budget(n)
  [OK] ...the value is the declared whole
  [OK] ...and Why() says a FAKE answered, so nothing reads as live
  [OK] a model that never complies REFUSES on exhaustion (LAW 3)
  [OK] ...and the refusal cites the field that failed
  [OK] ...and names the rule
  [OK] ...and the findings are readable afterwards, in the unified shape
  [OK] Budget(n) still bounds the retries -- it is not a suggestion
  [OK] ...and the refusal says the budget was what stopped it
  [OK] ...having spent exactly the budget, no more

-- Scene 8: a seed is judged like a generated answer --
  [OK] a valid seed answers, memoized and free
  [OK] an INVALID seed is refused at the door, not stored
  [OK] a seed given as RAW TEXT is parsed and validated like any answer

-- Scene 9: goldens hold structures --
  [OK] a structured golden PASSES when it agrees
  [OK] ...and fails when it does not
  [OK] ...naming the FIELD that moved, not just the record

-- Scene 10: STRUCTURE KILLS MALFORMEDNESS, NOT FALSEHOOD --
  [OK] a schema-valid LIE validates -- Ada Lovelace was not nine
  [OK] ...and the court has no complaint to make about it
  (so what is promised is INTEGRITY -- the shape asked for, whole, or
   nothing. Truth needs reversibility, audit and judgement, none of
   which live in a validator.)

